WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.270
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.769
This paper reveals us a fundamental difference
between how humans and machines learn.

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:13.379
Imagine the following situation: you are given
a video game with no instructions, you start

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:18.640
playing it, and the only information you get
is a line of text when you successfully finished

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.000
the game.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
That's it!

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.519
So far, so good, this is relatively easy to
play because the visual cues are quite clear:

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:32.579
the pink blob looks like an adversary, and
what the spikes do is also self-explanatory.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:36.629
This is easy to understand, so we can finish
the game in less than a minute.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:37.629
Easy!

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:40.030
Now, let's play this.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.250
Whoa!

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.510
What is happening?

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.489
Even empty space looks like as if it were
a solid tile.

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:50.940
I am not sure if I can finish this version
of the game, at least not in a minute for

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:51.940
sure.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:57.660
So, what is happening here is that some of
artwork of the objects has been masked out.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.260
As a result, this version of the game is much
harder to play for humans.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.600
So far, this is hardly surprising, and if
that would be it, this wouldn't have been

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.470
a very scientific experiment.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.750
However, this is not the case.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:16.170
So to proceed from this point, we will try
to find what makes humans learn so efficiently,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:22.299
but not by changing everything at once, but
by trying to change and measure only one variable

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:23.500
at a time.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.850
How about this version of the game?

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.850
This is still manageable, since the environment
remains the same, only the objects we interact

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:32.750
with have been masked.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.740
Through trial and error, we can find out the
mechanics of the game.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.620
What about reversing the semantics?

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:45.890
Spikes now became tasty ice cream, and the
shiny gold conceals an enemy that eats us.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.500
Very apt, I have to say.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.280
Again, with this, the problem suddenly became
more difficult for humans as we need some

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.610
trial and error to find out the rules.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:02:00.090
After putting together several other masking
strategies, they measured amount of time,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.090
the number of deaths and interactions that
were required to finish the level.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:09.750
I will draw your attention mainly to the blue
lines which show which variable caused how

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.900
much degradation in the performance of humans.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.680
The main piece of insight is not only that
these different visual cues throw off humans,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.880
but it tells us variable by variable, and
also, by how much.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:27.810
An important insight here is that highlighting
important objects and visual consistency are

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:28.940
key.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.130
So what about the machines?

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.770
How are learning algorithms affected?

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.440
These are the baseline results.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.520
Adding masked semantics?

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.780
Barely an issue.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.420
Masked object identities?

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.780
This sounds quite hard, right?

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.200
Barely an issue.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.330
Masked platforms and ladders?

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:47.410
Barely an issue.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:52.010
This is a remarkable property of learning
algorithms, as they don't only think in terms

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.320
of visual cues, but in terms of mathematics
and probabilities.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.490
Removing similarity information throws the
machines off a bit, which is understandable,

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.320
because the same objects may appear as if
they were completely different.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.420
There is more analysis on this in the paper,
so make sure to have a look.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.140
So, what are the conclusions here?

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.690
Humans are remarkably good at reusing knowledge,
and reading and understanding visual cues.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:23.310
However, if the visual cues become more cryptic,
their performance drastically decreases.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:28.840
When machines start playing the game, at first,
they have no idea which character they control,

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.620
how gravity works or how to defeat enemies,
or that keys are required to open doors.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.320
However, they learn these tricky problems
and games much easier and quicker, because

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.760
these mind-bending changes, as you remember,
are barely an issue.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.060
Note that you can play the original and the
obfuscated versions on the author's website

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.060
as well.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.530
Also note that we really have only scratched
the surface here, the paper contains a lot

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:55.620
more insights.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.060
So, it is the perfect time to nourish your
mind with a paper, so make sure to click it

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.290
in the video description and give it a read.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.730
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

